In this experiment the agent and \ac{pa} have \ac{los} connection throughout the whole trajectory. The results are displayed in Fig.~<<CMD_814>>a - <<CMD_815>>d. The eCDF of the agent's position error in Fig.~<<CMD_816>>c and the \ac{rmse} in Fig.~<<CMD_817>>d show similar results for the proposed algorithm and particle-based \ac{mpslam} with <<MATH_INLINE_641>> particles, both of them almost reaching the CRLB. The mean OSPA of all VAs (Fig.~<<CMD_818>>a) is higher for the proposed algorithm when compared to the <<MATH_INLINE_642>> and <<MATH_INLINE_643>> particle versions, which can be attributed to a higher mean cardinality error displayed in Fig.~<<CMD_819>>b. The <<MATH_INLINE_644>>-particle implementation leads to an agent estimation error larger than <<MATH_INLINE_645>> in <<MATH_INLINE_646>> of cases. The differences in runtime are significant, with the proposed algorithm being about <<MATH_INLINE_647>> times faster than the particle-based \ac{mpslam} with <<MATH_INLINE_648>> particles and around <<MATH_INLINE_649>> times faster for <<MATH_INLINE_650>> particles. Comparable runtimes could be achieved using <<MATH_INLINE_651>> particles, which, however, leads to a total loss of the agent's trajectory in all <<MATH_INLINE_652>> realizations.
