\subsubsection*{Experiment 2}
The scenario is displayed in Fig.~<<CMD_820>>, where a wall obstructs the LOS connection to the PA as well as to some VAs over some parts of the trajectory. The best result is achieved by the particle-based \ac{mpslam} with <<MATH_INLINE_658>> particles, as displayed in Fig.~<<CMD_821>> (c) and (e). For the proposed algorithm the agent position is lost in <<MATH_INLINE_659>> of realizations, which were removed from the RMSE plot in Fig.~<<CMD_822>>(e). Execution times are in close correspondence to Ex. 1 and are listed in Tab.~<<CMD_823>>.

\subsection{Measurement Results}
Measurements were conducted in the NXP laboratory room at TU Graz shown in Fig.~<<CMD_824>>, with one PA equipped with an antenna array and the agent having a single antenna, making it a multiple input, single output (MISO) scenario. Fig.~<<CMD_825>> also provides the agent's trajectory, which consists of a total of <<MATH_INLINE_660>> steps spaced approximately <<MATH_INLINE_661>> apart. 
<<COMMENT_267>>
Reference measurements were taken using an optical motion capture system from Qualisys, which provides ground truth measurements with an accuracy in the order of millimetres. The \ac{pa} is equipped with a <<MATH_INLINE_662>> phased-array with field of view of <<MATH_INLINE_663>> and a <<MATH_INLINE_664>> beamwidth of <<MATH_INLINE_665>>, with the beam steered in steps of <<MATH_INLINE_666>>. The limited field of view results in only two of the four walls being fully visible and parts of the room being invisible (see Fig.~<<CMD_826>>). The agent is represented by an antenna with omnidirectional radiation pattern in the horizontal plane and negligible radiation in vertical direction, making ground and ceiling reflections unlikely. Measurements were made using an Ilmens M-sequence direct correlation channel sounder operating at a carrier frequency of <<MATH_INLINE_667>>. The pulse shape is given by a raised-cosine pulse with a <<MATH_INLINE_668>> <<MATH_INLINE_669>>-bandwidth and a roll-off factor of <<MATH_INLINE_670>>. A total of <<MATH_INLINE_671>> samples were used limiting the maximum observable distance to <<MATH_INLINE_672>>.
